clearcote: versioned persona schema. Schema 1 = the original derivation, frozen and default;
schema 2 (--fingerprint-schema=2) couples screen class and GPU class to the hardware tier and
sizes performance.memory.jsHeapSizeLimit from the persona's RAM the way V8 does.

Why versioned: coupling changes what a seed derives, and a seed's identity is a customer's
stored session. Schema 1 is byte-for-byte unchanged for every seed (proven: 6000 personas, 3
platforms, identical before/after); schema 2 is a separate identity space a launch opts into.

Schema 2: per-tier screen pools (the kScreens marginals restricted to what a tier ships with,
renormalised -- no new marginal); per-tier GPU class (Intel iGPU / NVIDIA; AMD deliberately
absent until its class is decided), drawn always but honoured only when the launcher declared a
real GPU backend (--fingerprint-gpu-backend-real) or a canvas bridge -- a discrete claim the
host's pixels cannot back is worse than a plain one; heap limit = V8's sizing from physical
memory (old generation physical/2 desktop or /4 Android, clamped [256 MiB, 4 GiB], 256 KiB
pages; young generation from the semispace cap: 96 MiB desktop, 24 MiB non-high-end Android),
with physical = nominal RAM minus a seed-derived reserved slice (64..320 MiB), sized last from
the final RAM so the Android device table and an imported capture are respected. 16 GB and
above reproduce the 4,395,630,592 real Chrome reports; 8 GB and 4 GB personas no longer claim a
heap larger than their RAM. Validator: tier/screen/GPU coupling invariants and a V8 plausibility
band for the heap. Both switches are forwarded to renderers.

